<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John the Fearless</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1371">1371</a>–<a href="/years/1419">1419</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as John I, Duke of Burgundy, John the Fearless of Burgundy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John the Fearless was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 1404 until 1419, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>. Captured at Nicopolis in 1396 and victor over Liège at Othée in 1408, he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis of Orléans</a> murdered in 1407, which began the civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> and Armagnacs. He was himself murdered on the bridge at Montereau in 1419 by followers of the Dauphin, which drove his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> into alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless.jpg" alt="John the Fearless" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / After Jean Malouel / School of Jan van Eyck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_II,_Duke_of_Burgundy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was born at the Palace of the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> on 28 May 1371, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-iii-countess-of-flanders">Margaret III, Countess of Flanders</a>. His paternal grandfather was King John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which made him a prince of the blood. In 1384 he received the County of Nevers, and in 1385 he married Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in a double wedding at Cambrai that consolidated his position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><p>He led the French forces sent to aid Sigismund, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Sultan Bayezid I</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a> on 25 September 1396 fought so bravely that he was given the name Fearless. His impetuous leadership ended in disaster; he was captured and freed only the next year after a very large ransom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>John inherited the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> on 27 April 1404 and the counties of Burgundy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Artois on his mother's death in 1405. He at once clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</a>, the younger brother of the mentally ill King Charles VI, as both tried to fill the power vacuum. Though their uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-duke-of-berry">John, Duke of Berry</a>, secured a vow of reconciliation on 20 November 1407, Louis was killed in the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 23 November. John admitted the deed and called it a justifiable act of tyrannicide, and in the treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> of 9 March 1409 the king absolved him. Louis's son Charles allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-vii-count-of-armagnac">Bernard VII, Count of Armagnac</a>, and the factions became known as Armagnacs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, John kept in contact with him but, wary of losing his popularity, allied himself with the Armagnacs; his troops took no part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a> in 1415. On 30 May 1418 his troops captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the new Dauphin, the future Charles VII, escaping to the Bastille, and John made himself protector of the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Dauphin sought reconciliation, and the two swore peace on the bridge of Pouilly-le-Fort in July 1419. A second meeting was proposed for 10 September 1419 on the bridge at Montereau. John came with his escort, thinking it a diplomatic occasion, and was killed by the Dauphin's bodyguards, among them Tanneguy III du Châtel and Jean Louvet. He was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> in 1420. His son and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> then allied with the English, which prolonged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. John and his father also played a part in the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> artillery in European warfare.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John the Fearless” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1396 the future <b>John the Fearless</b> led the Burgundian contingent of the crusade destroyed by the Ottomans at the Battle of Nicopolis, where he was captured and later ransomed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Othée in 1408 <b>John the Fearless</b>, Duke of Burgundy, and his allies crushed a Liège army that had rebelled against the bishop-elect John of Bavaria, and thousands of Liègeois were killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>John, Duke of Berry, secured a vow of solemn reconciliation between his nephews, the rivals <b>John the Fearless</b>, Duke of Burgundy, and Louis I, Duke of Orléans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-20">20 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montereau-fault-yonne">Montereau-Fault-Yonne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.32,5.04&amp;year=1402" aria-label="Dijon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John the Fearless"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5292 6064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5292 6064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.32,5.04&amp;year=1402">What was here in 1402? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
